From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: shikosu <teovidal01@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 23:23:04 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] horizon on linux: textures of one memory are one image, and a
 texture can sample the stencil

A game makes several NVN textures on the same memory: one for the depth buffer and others to sample it (its depth, and its stencil, with nvnTextureBuilderSetDepthStencilMode(STENCIL)), one for a shadow map and one to read it. Each was its own GPU image, so the ones that were sampled were never written: in Xenoblade 2 every lit surface came out black (a silhouette) and the sea of clouds of the opening was missing. The textures of the same memory, format and size now share one image. A texture whose depth-stencil mode is STENCIL (the builder's, or the view's nvnTextureViewSetDepthStencilMode) is sampled through the stencil aspect (Nx2Tex.aspect, linux/host/gpu.c), which the deferred lighting pass reads as raw bits to select the pixels it lights.

NX2LINUX_GPU_DUMP now writes R11G11B10F, RGB10A2, RGBA16F and R32F targets and tells what depth images hold; NX2LINUX_GPU_DUMP_ALL (every image of 128x128 or more), NX2LINUX_GPU_DUMP_FROM, NX2LINUX_DRAW_LOG_FROM, and a log line for each shader that is not translated.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

Upstream-Status: pending
Reason: NX2Linux S7bis: the 3D scenes of Xenoblade 2 were black silhouettes
Issue: none yet (NX2Linux, branch swann/jalon-s7bis-xc2)
